CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ handler sees the thrown value: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG must FAIL: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [except.throw]/3-4, [except.handle]/1-3,15: throwing transfers control to
the matching handler and initializes the handler parameter from the exception
object, so `try { throw 42; } catch(int e)` enters the handler with e == 42.

Regression test for the goto-level C++ exception lowering (remove_cpp_exceptions,
run from process_goto_program): CATCH-PUSH/CATCH-POP/THROW are turned into
ordinary gotos and assignments before symex, so the thrown value reaches the
matching handler and the handler body is reachable.  assertion.1 checks the
handler sees the thrown value (non-vacuous: only holds if the handler is reached
and bound); assertion.2 is a deliberately-wrong assertion that must FAIL.
Previously goto-symex stubbed throw as assume(false), so both assertions passed
vacuously.
